## Series context & the key point _(why this concept, not a generic one)_

Part 1.1 is the **foundation of a 4-part Fit arc** (1: the goal → 2: structure of a day → 3: the program → 4: pharmacology). The author is explicit: "If the goal-setting is wrong, no protocol downstream will fix it." So this article's job is to install a _mental operating system_, not give a workout.

It does three things in sequence: (1) separates **Fit** (KPIs: how you look + perform) from **Healthy** (biomarkers); (2) collapses fitness down to the only two KPIs the average lifter should chase — **Aesthetic + Strength**; (3) — and this is the part the author personally flags `==Super Important==` — shows that once you track the numbers, **the _combination_ of your weight trend and strength trend becomes a diagnosis.** You stop guessing why a phase isn't working and start reading your body like an instrument.

**The single key point to make a reader feel:** _Two signals, read together, remove all ambiguity about what your body is doing._ And a quiet second lesson the visual can teach for free: **strength is the master signal** — notice that whenever strength is rising, you're almost always fine. That is the article's "strength is the honest proxy for size" thesis, made visible.

This is the concept other chats miss: they'd diagram the four benchmarks or draw a six-pack. The _real_ payload of this specific article is the diagnostic logic.

